UID:228@default
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Register now to take part in this inspirational discussion!&nbsp;LSC | Watch | Virtual Author Talks from the South Berwick Public LibraryJoin us for an exhilarating journey through the history of US women astronauts with Valerie Neal, emerita curator from the Smithsonian's National Air and Space Museum and expert on human spaceflight.Sally Ride became a household name as the first American woman in space, but scores of equally impressive women have also left their mark in space. On a Mission: The Smithsonian History of US Women Astronauts spans 45 years and 61 astronauts to share the epic journeys of women who made space for themselves in a male-dominated field.Valerie Neal interviewed many of the US women astronauts to bring their experiences to life. She offers a culturally insightful history of their achievements, the challenges they've faced, and their distinctive stories. Collectively, they've completed more than 100 space shuttle missions, and more than 30 long-duration stays on the International Space Station and Russian Space Station Mir, and they continue to prove themselves in present-day space exploration efforts.The book includes 50 black-and-white photographs to complement the historical account. With its sweeping look from the first women astronauts to Christina Hammock Koch, assigned to the first crewed Artemis mission around the Moon, there is no comparably thorough book on America's women astronauts. On a Mission is an inspiring tribute to unsung women's history.
DTSTAMP;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20261111T111128Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260402T180000Z
DTEND;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260402T190000Z
LOCATION:none
SUMMARY;LANGUAGE=en-us:On a Mission: The History of US Women Astronauts with Smithsonian Curator Emerita Valerie Neal
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:229@default
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Register and ask questions of the author here:LSC | Watch | Virtual Author Talks from the South Berwick Public LibraryJoin us in conversation with acclaimed author Brad Taylor on his latest work, Shadow Strike, book 20 (yes, you read that right!) of the bestselling Pike Logan series.&nbsp;After its proxies are devastated and its offensive capability pummeled in the latest war in the Middle East, a rogue group of Iranian regime officials create a brazen plot to strike back at their hated enemies once and for all. They envision a series of operational dominos culminating in a devastating attack, and the first step is the assassination of the Israeli prime minister. &nbsp;And there&rsquo;s only one assassin with the skills to pull it off: Abdul Rahman, known in the shadows as the Ghost.&nbsp;When a routine prison transfer is ambushed, the Ghost escapes and is given the assignment. The only Operator who can hunt him down is the man who stopped him before: Pike Logan.Pike and his team soon learn that the mission involves something bigger than just the escape of his old enemy. Working with Mossad agents, the pursuit leads the Taskforce to Argentina. They work to unravel the scope of the attack, and the chase leads them through the tempestuous waterfalls of Iguazu and the Triple Frontier, to the vibrant streets of Buenos Aires, and the tiny village of Ushuaia at the &ldquo;End of the World."&nbsp;As the team races against the clock, Pike learns the stakes are much greater than a single life &ndash; the consequences extend into the heartland of America itself. The Ghost may hold the key to an escalation that will upend the worldwide balance of power, and if Pike fails, the fallout won&rsquo;t just be personal &ndash; it&rsquo;ll be global.
DTSTAMP;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20261111T111128Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260414T230000Z
DTEND;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260415T000000Z
LOCATION:none
SUMMARY;LANGUAGE=en-us:On Writing Action-Packed and Suspenseful Spycraft with Brad Taylor
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:230@default
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Register now to join in on the online conversation about the final installment to Jason Reynold&rsquo;s Track series!LSC | Watch | Virtual Author Talks from the South Berwick Public LibraryJoin us in virtual conversation with bestselling author Jason Reynolds as we discuss his latest work, Coach, the fifth&ndash;and last&ndash;entry in the highly popular Track series. Following the immersive stories of Ghost, Lu, Patina, and Sunny, it&rsquo;s finally time for Coach to shine in his own book as we discover: Who was Coach before he became an inspiring leader?Before Coach was the man who gave caring yet firm-handed guidance to Ghost, Lu, Patina, and Sunny on the Defenders track team, he was little Otie Brody, who was obsessed with Mr. 9.99 (a.k.a. Carl Lewis) and Marty McFly from Back to the Future. Like Mr. 9.99&mdash;and his own dad&mdash;Otie is a sprinter. Sprint free or die is practically his motto.Then his dad, who is always away on business trips, comes home with a pair of Jordans. JORDANS. Fine as fine can be. Otie puts them on and feels like he can leap to the moon&hellip;maybe even leap like Mr. 9.99 when he won the Olympic gold medal in the long jump. But one morning he wakes up to find his brand-new secret weapon kicks are missing&mdash;right off his feet! And Otie just might have a fuzzy memory of his dad easing them off as Otie was sleeping, but that can&rsquo;t be right, can it?Unless all the reasons for his dad&rsquo;s &ldquo;gone&rsquo;s&rdquo; are very different from what he&rsquo;s been told&hellip; Because now, not only are the Jordans missing, but so is his father.
DTSTAMP;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20261111T111128Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260430T230000Z
DTEND;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260501T000000Z
LOCATION:none
SUMMARY;LANGUAGE=en-us:Jason Reynolds: On Writing Beloved Characters and Powerful Portrayals of Resilience
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:231@default
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Shawn Jalbert of Native Haunts, this talk explores why native plants are essential to the health of Maine&rsquo;s ecosystems and how homeowners can play a direct role in conservation right in their own backyards. We&rsquo;ll look at the ecological benefits of native species, both plants and insects, and discuss practical ways to welcome them into our home landscapes. Scratching your head about what to plant after removing invasive species? We will highlight readily available native plants-trees, shrubs, and perennials that restore habitat, increase biodiversity, and create resilient, beautiful outdoor spaces.This event is a hybrid event.&nbsp; You may attend in-person at the library or virtually on Zoom.&nbsp; To watch the Zoom event, click here.This event is sponsored by the Great Works Regional Land Trust and the South Berwick Conservation Commission and hosted by the South Berwick Public Library.For more information, call the library at 207-384-3308.
DTSTAMP;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20261111T111128Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260421T213000Z
DTEND;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260421T223000Z
LOCATION:South Berwick Public Library
SUMMARY;LANGUAGE=en-us:From Yard to Habitat: How to Restore Native Plants to Your Yard.&nbsp; In-Person and Virtual Options
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
